# How to use this lesson

Lesson 39 promised that the Dirac Hamiltonian's nonrelativistic reduction gives g=2. Lesson 43 computed α/(2π)≈0.001161 and said it was Schwinger's a=(g−2)/2. Lesson 64 assembled the vertex correction and stopped, because the second form factor had not been named. This lesson names it, evaluates F2(0) at one loop, and obtains a=α/(2π). The full Feynman-parameter integral is reduced here at q²=0 to ∫du(1−u), after the Gordon projection; the Dirac-algebra steps that isolate that integrand from Lesson 64's numerator are the content of Exercises 16's challenge and of Section 3. Expect eight to ten hours.

## Learning objectives

- Write the on-shell vertex as ū(p′)Γ^μ u(p)=ū(p′)[γ^μ F1(q²)+iσ^{μν}q_ν F2(q²)/(2m)]u(p), with q=p′−p.

- Identify F1(0)=1 as charge (after renormalization) and F2(0)=a=(g−2)/2 as the anomalous magnetic moment.

- Use the Gordon identity to see how a magnetic-moment term can hide inside a γ^μ vertex.

- Derive g=2 by squaring the Dirac Hamiltonian of Lesson 39, using [π̂_i,π̂_j]=iq ε_ijk B_k.

- Reduce the one-loop F2 integral at q²=0 to (α/π)∫₀¹ du(1−u)=α/(2π).

- Distinguish this finite number from the ultraviolet divergence in F1, which was absorbed into charge renormalization.

