# 1 g=2 from the Dirac Hamiltonian, derived

Lesson 39 postponed the nonrelativistic expansion of h_D. It is done here, because Lesson 75's whole point is what sits on top of g=2.

## 1.1 Squaring

For a particle of charge q, π̂ = p̂ − qA, and Lesson 39's h_D − qφ = α·π̂ + βm. Square, using {α_i,α_j}=2δ_ij, {α_i,β}=0, and β²=1:

$$ (α · π̂ + β m)² = (α · π̂)² + m²

In the Dirac representation α_i α_j = δ_ij + i ε_ijk Σ_k, with Σ=diag(σ,σ), so (α·π̂)² = π̂² + i Σ·(π̂×π̂). The operator identity [π̂_i, π̂_j] = i q ε_ijk B_k implies (π̂×π̂)_k = i q B_k, and therefore i Σ·(π̂×π̂) = −q Σ·B:

$$ (α · π̂)² = π̂² − q Σ · B

## 1.2 The nonrelativistic piece

Expanding √(m² + π̂² − q Σ·B) = m + (π̂² − q Σ·B)/(2m) + ⋯, the large-component Hamiltonian is π̂²/2m − (q/2m) σ·B. A magnetic moment μ = g (q/2m) S with S=σ/2 would have produced −(g q/4m) σ·B. Matching coefficients gives g=2, with no free parameter. For the electron, q=−e, the same g=2 holds; radiative corrections then add a.

### Worked example 1

If the interaction is −μ·B with μ=g (q/2m) S and S=σ/2, what g corresponds to −(q/2m)σ·B? Comparing −(q/2m)σ·B = −g(q/2m)(σ/2)·B gives g=2.

# 2 Two form factors

