The most general Lorentz-covariant, parity-conserving vertex between on-shell Dirac spinors, built from p, p′, and γ^μ, can be reduced with the Dirac equation to two scalar functions of q²:

$$ ū(p′) Γ^μ u(p) = ū(p′) [ γ^μ F₁(q²) + i σ^(μν) q_ν F₂(q²) / (2m) ] u(p)

F1(0) is the electric charge in units of e, after renormalization F1(0)=1. F2(0) multiplies the magnetic moment beyond the Dirac value: a=F2(0).

The Gordon identity,

$$ ū(p′) γ^μ u(p) = ū(p′) [ (p+p′)^μ / (2m) + i σ^(μν) q_ν / (2m) ] u(p)

shows that even the tree vertex γ^μ already contains a magnetic piece, the one that produces g=2. Any extra F2 is an anomaly on top of that.

# 3 Isolating F2 from Lesson 64

## 3.1 Why the integral for F2 converges

Lesson 64's iΛ^μ has three propagators in the denominator, growing as k⁶, and a numerator that grows as k², so the diagram is logarithmically divergent. The divergence sits entirely in the coefficient of γ^μ, i.e. in F1. The Pauli structure iσ^{μν}q_ν already carries one power of the external momentum q; that extra q improves the large-k count by one and leaves a finite integral for F2.

## 3.2 Feynman parameters, then q²=0

Combine the three denominators by Lesson 66's postponed identity, ∫₀¹ dx dy dz δ(x+y+z−1) / (xk²+y(p'+k)²+z(p+k)²)³. Shift k so the denominator is (ℓ²−Δ)³. Lesson 66's Euclidean integral then produces a factor 1/Δ. Projecting onto iσ^{μν}q_ν with the Gordon identity of Section 2, and then setting q=0, collapses the remaining parameter integral to a single line:

$$ F₂(0) = (α / π) ∫_0^1 du (1 − u) = (α / π) [ u − u²/2 ]_0^1 = α / (2π)

The integrand (1−u) is not an ansatz: it is what remains of Lesson 64's numerator after the terms that produce γ^μ have been subtracted, at vanishing momentum transfer. Then a=F2(0)=α/(2π) and g=2+α/π=2(1+α/(2π)).

### Worked example 2

Compute g from α=1/137.036. a=1/(2π×137.036)=0.0011614, g=2.0023228, matching Lesson 43, Exercise 13. The experimental electron anomaly is 0.00115965218073(28) (CODATA), so this one-loop term accounts for all but about 0.15% of a; the rest is higher loops and a tiny hadronic piece (Lesson 78). Schwinger's letter is Phys. Rev. 73, 416 (1948).

# 4 Why F2(0) could not have been absorbed

Lesson 67 absorbed divergences into parameters already in the Lagrangian. There is no tree-level σ^{μν}F_μν ψ̄ψ operator in ℒ_QED. A finite F2(0) is therefore a prediction, not a quantity that can be hidden in e or m. That is why a=(g−2)/2 is a precision test rather than a renormalization condition.

